---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const care = assertion('the-most-dangerous-work-here-is-care')
const risk = assertion('the-refinery-is-safer-than-the-nursing-home')
const total = assertion('five-thousand-seven-hundred-recordable-injuries')
const covid = assertion('the-pandemic-is-in-the-injury-logs')
const trend = assertion('the-fall-is-real-and-the-trend-is-not')
const deaths = assertion('three-workplace-deaths-and-that-is-ordinary')
const ford = assertion('a-plant-headcount-arrived-in-a-safety-filing')
const reach = assertion('one-workplace-in-ten-owes-a-safety-filing')

const spec = (a: typeof care, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const careSpec = spec(care, 'Recordable cases per 100 full-time workers')
const riskSpec = spec(risk, 'Recordable cases per 100 full-time workers')
const trendSpec = spec(trend, 'Recordable cases in 2024')
---

<Article slug="the-work-that-replaced-the-work">
  <p>
    Allen County's factory employment fell by more than half, health care passed manufacturing in
    2002 and led for twenty-one years, and in 2023 the two came level at about nine thousand
    employees each. That much is a familiar shape for a county in this part of Ohio. What the trade
    cost the people who do the work is a separate question, and the annual injury summaries that
    large employers file with OSHA answer it.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={care} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="care"
    title="Two sectors, nine years"
    caption="Recordable injuries and illnesses per 100 full-time workers, 2016 to 2024. Manufacturing worked sixty per cent more hours and recorded fewer cases."
    columns={['Sector', 'Rate']}
    rows={care.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(2)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="care" set:html={JSON.stringify(careSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Health care stands above manufacturing in every one of the nine years, and not narrowly: 5.10
    against 4.11 at the start of the run and 4.10 against 2.34 at the end. The sector that took the
    county's employment lead is the sector that sends people home hurt.
  </p>
  <p>
    A recordable case is one an employer's own log has to carry — anything past first aid, meaning
    lost time, restricted duty, a transfer, or treatment a nurse cannot give. It counts a nurse's
    back and a machinist's hand alike, which is what makes the two sectors comparable at all, and it
    counts neither the near miss nor the injury nobody reported. The rate sets that count against
    hours actually worked, so a sector cannot look safe by employing fewer people.
  </p>

  <h2>The safest place and the most dangerous</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={risk} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="risk"
    title="An oil refinery and a nursing home"
    caption="Nine years of filings from each. The nine contractor firms working on the refinery's ground record 0.77, so the low figure is not the result of contracting the hazard out."
    columns={['Workplace', 'Rate']}
    rows={risk.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(2)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="risk" set:html={JSON.stringify(riskSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The reasonable guess about a refinery's low rate is that its dangerous work is done by
    contractors and recorded on somebody else's logbook. The file says otherwise. Nine contractor
    firms filing from the same address record nine cases on 2,324,430 hours — the same rate as the
    plant's own crews.
  </p>
  <p>
    What separates the two is the kind of hazard rather than the amount of care taken. A refinery's
    risks are rare and catastrophic, and a rate built from how often somebody is hurt cannot see
    them at all. A nursing home's are frequent and ordinary: lifting people who cannot lift
    themselves, and being struck by the ones who would rather not be lifted.
  </p>

  <h2>What nine years cost</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={total} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={deaths} />
  <p>
    Three deaths in nine years reads as a small number and is exactly the number this many hours of
    work produces at the national rate. Reporting it as a finding would be reading noise; leaving it
    out would be worse. The three were at a tank fabricator in 2016, an electroplating shop in 2019
    and Ford's engine plant in 2021 — all three in manufacturing, in a county whose worst injury
    rates are somewhere else entirely.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={covid} />
  <p>
    The pandemic is legible here in a way it is legible almost nowhere else in this county's record.
    A respiratory illness contracted at work is recordable, and the hospitals and nursing homes
    recorded them: 211 of the 216 the county logged in 2020.
  </p>

  <h2>Which way it is going</h2>
  <p>
    The county sat above Ohio's rate in each year from 2016 to 2020 and below it in each year since,
    which is a crossing worth noticing and is not, by itself, a direction. Two questions hide inside
    the sentence <em>the rate is falling</em>, and they have different answers.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={trend} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="trend"
    title="What 2024 would have been"
    caption="Cases the county's 2024 hours would have produced at 2019's rate, against the cases actually recorded."
    columns={['Measure', 'Cases']}
    rows={trend.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="trend" set:html={JSON.stringify(trendSpec)} is:inline />
  <Gloss>
    The published 2024 rate is 1.80, and it is wrong. One Lima nursing home reported 20,482,105
    hours worked for 163 employees — 125,657 hours each, where a year holds 8,760 — against 204,728
    hours the year before for 162. A hundredfold slip in one field, and a third of everything the
    county's employers reported that year. The national file is worse: in 2019 an Arkansas
    establishment with seven employees reported sixteen trillion hours, enough on its own to take
    the national rate anyone could compute from the published file to 0.016 against a true 4.02.
    Every error of this kind inflates a denominator, so every one makes workplaces look safer.
  </Gloss>

  <h2>What the file reaches</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={reach} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ford} />
  <p>
    A workplace under the reporting threshold files nothing whatever happens in it, so none of this
    is a count of how many people were hurt at work in Allen County. What it is instead is a
    look inside the logbooks of the large end of the county's economy — including, incidentally, the
    only public statement of how many people work at the engine plant on Bible Road.
  </p>
</Article>
